// A coarse timeframe trend read, drawn over a fine timeframe chart, stating in
// the source which of the three readings it takes.
//
// Exercises: req.timeframe and its repaint mode, an expression evaluated on the
// folded bars rather than on the chart's, barColor and background driven by a
// regime, and a flip marker that compares a top level name with its own past.

version 1

study("Higher timeframe bias", overlay = true, precision = 2)

biasTf  = input("1D", "Bias timeframe", kind = "interval")
biasLen = input(20,   "Bias average length", min = 2, max = 500)
paint   = input(true, "Recolour the candles")

// mode is a literal and not an input, deliberately. The three readings are:
//
//   "confirmed"  the last coarse bar that has finished, held constant across
//                the one now forming. It never uses a bar that had not happened
//                yet, so what is on the chart today is what was on it at the
//                time.
//   "developing" the coarse bar as it stands on this bar: its high so far, its
//                close so far. Also honest, but the value changes while the
//                coarse bar is still open, so a signal taken from it can be
//                withdrawn.
//   "lookahead"  the finished values of the coarse bar, on every bar inside it.
//                This reads bars from the future and repaints. It exists so
//                that a study written elsewhere can be reproduced, not because
//                it is a defensible way to trade.
//
// "confirmed" is the default and the only one of the three that never repaints,
// which is why a read that says nothing cannot.
//
// One of the other two is warned about, not both. "lookahead" carries OS8005,
// and it also marks the compiled study as repainting so the host can say so in
// the legend, because a warning in an editor nobody opens again is not a
// disclosure. "developing" carries no warning of its own: the mode word written
// out on the line is its disclosure, which is the whole reason it has to be
// written out.
//
// OS8002 is a different rule and is about this file rather than about a mode. It
// is reported on a higher timeframe read in a file that sets
// onUnconfirmed = true, whatever that read's mode, because that pair repaints
// the confirmed history as well.
//
// Making this a setting would let a reader change the honesty of the study
// without reading it, which is exactly the thing the mode is here to prevent.
biasClose   = req.timeframe(biasTf, close, mode = "confirmed")
biasAverage = req.timeframe(biasTf, ema(close, biasLen), mode = "confirmed")

// The average is computed from the folded bars, not from the chart's bars: a 20
// period average of daily closes, sampled onto every five minute bar, which is
// not the same series as a 20 period average of five minute closes.
up   = not isNone(biasAverage) and biasClose > biasAverage
down = not isNone(biasAverage) and biasClose < biasAverage

// A step plot, because the value changes once per coarse bar and a straight
// line sloping between two daily readings would imply intraday values that were
// never read.
plot(biasAverage, "Bias average", orange, width = 2, style = "step")

barColor(paint ? (up ? lime : down ? red : none) : none)
background(up ? fade(lime, 95) : down ? fade(red, 95) : none)

// This file does not set onUnconfirmed, so signals wait for the bar to close.
// Setting it here would make the compiler report OS8002 on the two reads above,
// and it would be right to: an unconfirmed fine bar reading a coarse bar is
// where repainting comes from even when the mode is honest.
if up and not orElse(up[1], false)
    signal("BIAS UP")

if down and not orElse(down[1], false)
    signal("BIAS DOWN")
